WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.024 --> 00:00:09.580
Eleanor Roosevelt thought she was ugly. Her mother was disappointed in Eleanor’s looks and even called her "Granny" because she was

2
00:00:09.581 --> 00:00:13.350
quiet, serious, and awkward. This sort of treatment made the young girl shy and insecure. Yet she eventually became a skilled public

3
00:00:13.351 --> 00:00:17.500
speaker and world leader. She spent her adult life fighting for human rights, and even though her insecurity never left her,

4
00:00:17.501 --> 00:00:27.989
Eleanor Roosevelt became the most admired and influential woman of her time. Born in New York City, New York, on October

5
00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:37.831
11, 1884, Anna Eleanor Roosevelt belonged to one of the most important families in the United States. Her father was the

6
00:00:37.832 --> 00:00:48.216
younger brother of Theodore Roosevelt, president of the United States from 1901 to 1909, but the family’s prominence couldn’t protect her from

7
00:00:48.217 --> 00:00:54.652
tragedy. Her mother died when Eleanor was eight, and her father, who was an alcoholic, died less than two years later.

8
00:00:54.653 --> 00:00:58.693
She spent the majority of her childhood with her stern grandmother. Like other wealthy children in New York, Eleanor was taught

9
00:00:58.694 --> 00:01:06.153
by a private tutor; therefore, she did not make many friends. It wasn’t until she was sent to a boarding school in

10
00:01:06.154 --> 00:01:12.742
England at age 15 that she found people who accepted her. Marie Souvestre, the school’s progressive headmistress, taught her students to

11
00:01:12.743 --> 00:01:20.225
think for themselves and express their opinions. She took Eleanor on trips during summer breaks, instilling in her a sense of

12
00:01:20.226 --> 00:01:27.550
independence and social responsibility. During her three years at the school, Eleanor found she was popular among the students and, for

13
00:01:27.551 --> 00:01:36.162
the first time, began to believe in herself. When she returned to New York, Eleanor was expected to "come out" into society&#8212;to

14
00:01:36.163 --> 00:01:44.544
be formally presented as a woman who is eligible for marriage. Eleanor attended balls and other high-society events, but she preferred

15
00:01:44.545 --> 00:01:51.313
to spend her time in community service. She volunteered in the slums of New York, teaching immigrant women and women who

16
00:01:51.314 --> 00:01:57.519
worked in clothing factories. During this time, she learned about the terrible living conditions these women endured. When she became acquainted with

17
00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:02.193
Franklin Delano Roosevelt, her fifth cousin, she introduced him to the dreadful lives of immigrants and the poor. Franklin liked that

18
00:02:02.194 --> 00:02:10.307
Eleanor was intelligent and not afraid to speak her mind. On March 17, 1905, Eleanor and Franklin married, with President Teddy

19
00:02:10.308 --> 00:02:14.527
Roosevelt giving the bride away. The young couple lived in New York City, and Eleanor’s life revolved around her family. In

20
00:02:14.528 --> 00:02:21.748
1906, she had her first child; she would have six children over the next ten years. In 1909, when one of her

21
00:02:21.749 --> 00:02:26.684
babies died, the experience brought not only heartache but renewed feelings of insecurity&#8212;she felt she was a failure as a mother.

22
00:02:26.685 --> 00:02:32.676
But Eleanor overcame her grief and continued in her role as mother and society wife. As Franklin took on different political

23
00:02:32.677 --> 00:02:42.614
jobs, the family moved to Albany, New York, in 1910, and later, to Washington DC, and Eleanor assumed the duties of a

24
00:02:42.615 --> 00:02:48.002
politician’s wife, hosting other politicians and national leaders. In 1917, during World War I, she joined the war effort by working

25
00:02:48.003 --> 00:03:01.929
for the Red Cross. She loved being useful and gained confidence in herself. Unfortunately, that confidence crumbled in 1918 when Eleanor

26
00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:11.715
found love letters from her social secretary to her husband. This experience made her feel like a failure again&#8212;this time as

27
00:03:11.716 --> 00:03:19.201
a wife&#8212;but it also became a turning point in Eleanor’s life. Although she continued to live with Franklin, she threw herself into

28
00:03:19.202 --> 00:03:26.741
volunteer work to get over the hurt of his infidelity and made friends with women who, like her, were interested in

29
00:03:26.742 --> 00:03:41.736
obtaining equal pay, better working conditions, and more work opportunities for women. Eleanor’s world came crashing down again when Franklin was

30
00:03:41.737 --> 00:03:52.462
paralyzed by polio in the summer of 1921. Despite their problems, Eleanor nursed Franklin tirelessly. While he recovered, she also kept the

31
00:03:52.463 --> 00:04:00.264
Roosevelt name before the public, representing her husband until he could return to politics. Guided by Franklin’s political adviser, she became

32
00:04:00.265 --> 00:04:12.221
a skilled public speaker and worked for the New York State Democratic Party, convincing the Democrats to support child-labor laws and

33
00:04:12.222 --> 00:04:21.922
equal pay for women. During this time, and as Franklin returned to politics, becoming governor of New York in 1928, a

34
00:04:21.923 --> 00:04:29.485
new partnership emerged between Eleanor and Franklin. Eleanor reported to her disabled husband on what she saw and heard while inspecting government

35
00:04:29.486 --> 00:04:35.408
facilities such as hospitals and prisons. Once he became president of the United States in 1932, that partnership blossomed. She acted

36
00:04:35.409 --> 00:04:41.734
as the conscience of his administration during the dark times of the Great Depression and World War II. Franklin often pushed

37
00:04:41.735 --> 00:04:46.118
for programs because of what Eleanor had observed. For example, Eleanor urged the creation of the National Youth Administration, which provided job

38
00:04:46.119 --> 00:04:57.567
training and work for young people. Eleanor and Franklin became an unbeatable team for change. Eleanor was the most politically involved

39
00:04:57.568 --> 00:05:02.662
first lady the United States had ever seen. She even held her own press conferences&#8212;some for women reporters only, to push

40
00:05:02.663 --> 00:05:09.611
newspapers to hire more women. Starting in 1936, she also shared her ideas six days a week with millions of people

41
00:05:09.612 --> 00:05:17.889
in a newspaper column titled "My Day." She spoke out for civil rights, saying that America could not fight Hitler’s racism abroad

42
00:05:17.890 --> 00:05:27.041
while allowing racism against blacks in the United States. She led by example; when a women’s society to which she belonged

43
00:05:27.042 --> 00:05:39.848
would not let black singer Marian Anderson perform in its building, she resigned from the group and helped organize a concert

44
00:05:39.849 --> 00:05:48.009
for Anderson at the Lincoln Memorial. Eleanor’s involvement in politics left her open to attack. She was criticized for her fight against

45
00:05:48.010 --> 00:05:59.344
segregation and her interest in the poor. Many believed that her defense of the right to free speech was un-American and

46
00:05:59.345 --> 00:06:05.288
made her a threat to the country. And because of her support for liberal organizations, her critics said she was a

47
00:06:05.289 --> 00:06:14.775
communist. The FBI followed all her activities closely and built a three-thousand-page file on her! Franklin and Eleanor Roosevelt’s political partnership

48
00:06:14.776 --> 00:06:23.641
ended on April 12, 1945, when Franklin died, but Eleanor continued her work. She was chosen to be a delegate to the

49
00:06:23.642 --> 00:06:29.308
newly formed United Nations and to lead a commission responsible for writing the Universal Declaration of Human Rights. This declaration was

50
00:06:29.309 --> 00:06:38.078
designed to prevent the horrors of World War II from ever occurring again. Under her leadership, the committee produced a document

51
00:06:38.079 --> 00:06:44.356
that is still honored by most nations of the world; she considered it her greatest accomplishment. Eleanor Roosevelt died from a rare

52
00:06:44.357 --> 00:06:51.282
form of tuberculosis on November 6, 1962. Until shortly before her death, she continued to travel and meet with world leaders

53
00:06:51.283 --> 00:06:57.179
and lecture and write extensively. She wanted to instill in people the ideal of giving to others in order to make

54
00:06:57.180 --> 00:07:05.010
the world a better place. She is remembered as a woman who lived that ideal&#8212;who fought to help the needy and

55
00:07:05.011 --> 00:07:10.280
made the world recognize the basic rights of individuals. She improved the lives of many and inspired countless others to follow in

56
00:07:10.281 --> 00:07:17.889
her footsteps. The shy, quiet girl was beloved as a brave, eloquent woman&#8212;she truly was the first lady of the world.

